<BaseLayout title="About — Eric Angella" description="About Eric Angella">
  <div class="about">
    <p class="section-label" style="padding-top: 60px;">About</p>
    <h1>More than the headline.</h1>
 <p><em>Want to work together? This is your best pre-introduction to me, what I do, and why. Your time is valuable, so I compressed it into a three minute read that covers everything.</em></p>

<hr class="rule" />

<p>In 1990, I rented a chisel and chipped off large chunks of the Berlin wall. I still have them, along with a few feet of barbed wire.</p>
<p>Iraq's army marched into Kuwait hours before my flight home, so I spent quality time at Heathrow airport explaining to officials why I have barbed wire stuffed in my backpack.</p>
<p>I have since built a career founded in four areas over three-plus decades. They might seem unrelated at first:</p>
<ul>
<li>Finance</li>
<li>Technology</li>
<li>Entertainment</li>
<li>Entrepreneurship</li>
</ul>
<p>I think of them as pillars in my <em>Adventure Portfolio</em>, my rocket fuel for what's next.</p>
<p>I borrowed the term from a Justin Welsh article titled <a href="https://justinwelsh.me/essays/the-portfolio-life-experiment">the portfolio life experiment</a>. He broke down the component parts of his career portfolio and labeled them as adventures.</p>
<p>The term stuck with me.</p>
<p>Justin's article is slightly longer than this one and worth a read if you have a multiple skills and interests, and want to package them into a valuable service offering with multiple income streams. Because not all of us are forged in the one-title, one-career-track mold.</p>
<p>A related article I wrote: <a href="https://justinwelsh.me/essays/the-portfolio-life-experiment">a portfolio life: career advice I'll give my young daughters when they're ready</a>.</p>
<p>So what does all this rocket fuel power in my case?</p>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong></p>
<p>I write for tech executives and startup founders to help clarify their thinking and order their thoughts into published articles. I also build inbound flywheels for startups and smaller companies built on a developed thought leadership layer.</p>
<p>With patience and my skill, the work leads to inbound revenue.</p>
<p><strong>Principles:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>I work directly with the person I write for.</li>
<li>My personal craft is used in critical places to keep it human (drop the slop).</li>
<li>AI is used to assist the thinking process, not do the thinking. AI is a rocket-pack, not a robot.</li>
<li>I use AI aggressively where it can help and where it can scale.</li>
<li>I hand off the AI-powered research, versioning, and drafting engine that I've customized for founders who want to publish on their own, and I say goodbye (unless we're building a flywheel).</li>
<li>I focus much effort on finding a unique voice.</li>
</ul>
<p>To that last point:</p>
<p>My office includes a fully-functioning podcast studio. A real one used in active YouTube channels.</p>
<p>A client that I ghostwrite for and his team flew cross-country to record a three-hour podcast. The transcript from that podcast 10x'd my source material to build a unique voice.</p>
<p>His published work is all the sharper, and more human, for it.</p>
<p>I'm constantly learning just how deep earned authority can be but hidden from view from the very people who could benefit from it.</p>
<p>Once that wall is blown apart, anything is possible.</p>

  </div>
</BaseLayout>

